{"subjectId":"data-science","id":"wordnet","title":"Synonyms & Antonyms with WordNet","group":"Natural Language Processing","order":20,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">WordNet is a dictionary for programs: words are grouped into sets of synonyms (<em>synsets</em>) with definitions, and linked to antonyms and broader or narrower words.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from nltk.corpus import wordnet\n\nsyn = wordnet.synsets(\"book\")\nprint(syn[0].definition())      # a written work or composition that has been published (printed on pages bound together)\nprint(syn[0].name())            # book.n.01</code></pre></figure>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-shuffle\"></i>Synonyms</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from nltk.corpus import wordnet\nsynonyms = []\nfor s in wordnet.synsets(\"happy\"):\n    for lemma in s.lemmas():\n        synonyms.append(lemma.name())\nprint(sorted(set(synonyms)))    # ['felicitous', 'glad', 'happy', 'well-chosen']</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>Use a <code>set</code> to drop duplicates — the notebook's list repeats “happy” four times because it appears in four synsets.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-diff\"></i>Antonyms</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from nltk.corpus import wordnet\nantonyms = {a.name() for s in wordnet.synsets(\"healthy\") for l in s.lemmas() for a in l.antonyms()}\nprint(sorted(antonyms))         # ['unhealthy']</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Call</th><th>Gives</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>wordnet.synsets(word)</code></td><td>Every sense of the word</td></tr><tr><td><code>synset.definition()</code> / <code>.examples()</code></td><td>Its meaning / example sentences</td></tr><tr><td><code>synset.lemmas()</code></td><td>The words in that sense (synonyms)</td></tr><tr><td><code>lemma.antonyms()</code></td><td>Opposites</td></tr><tr><td><code>synset.hypernyms()</code></td><td>Broader terms (dog → canine)</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section>"}